// P3.5 / PS9: appearances. One sketch with nine separate squares, one extrude: nine parts.
// 01 PS9.1 the parts take the 8-colour palette in the order they were made (the kernel numbers
//    them from the right-hand end: Part 1 at the far right, Part 8 at the origin); the ninth
//    starts it again (Part 9, second from the left, is Part 1's light blue).
// 02 right-clicking Part 3: its row gets the light context band (the part is not selected, it
//    keeps its colour in the view, `ex2-step8`); Assign material…, Edit appearance… and Make
//    transparent… are enabled, as in `ex1-step5`.
// 03 Edit appearance…, the fifth preset swatch, ✓: Part 3 is sand.
// 03b Part 3 selected: a warm part turns a deeper orange than the usual selection amber, so the
//    selection reads on sand and tan (P3.5 judge).
// 03c (P3.6) Part 3's top face selected: on the warm sand face the amber outline gets a dark core.
// 04 PS9.2 Part 1's Edit appearance…: the preset swatches (the palette first, Part 1's light blue
//    ringed), Custom colors with +, the Mixer (square and hue strip), the current colour, Hex and
//    R G B.
// 05 Hex "#D63031" + Enter: Part 1 is red live; the field lets go of the focus (no caret); the
//    mixer's knobs, the RGB values (214 48 49) and the ringed swatch follow.
// 06 PS9.3 Opacity pressed at about 43%: Part 1 turns translucent.
// 07 ✓: applied (one undo step); the dialog is gone.
// 08 PS9.4 right-clicking Part 2's top face: Add appearance to face…, Edit appearance…, Assign
//    material….
// 09 Add appearance to face… and the dark blue swatch: only that face is dark blue.
// 10 Part 4's Edit appearance…: a point in the hue strip and the square (a purple); + saves it as a
//    custom colour.
// 11 PS9.2 right-clicking the custom colour: Update color and Delete.
// 12 a green picked in the hue strip, then the custom colour's Update color: the saved swatch is
//    green now.
// 13 the custom colour's Delete: the row holds only +; ✓ gives Part 4 the green.
// 14 PS9.1 Delete Part 5 (the Parts list's Delete): the other parts keep their colours.
// 15 PS9.4 Extrude 1's Add appearance to feature…, grey: every face of the extrude is grey except
//    Part 2's top face (a face's own appearance wins over its feature's).
// 16 PS9.5 a second sketch on Front (a circle and a rectangle), Edit sketch appearance… from its
//    row, the green swatch: its curves are green.
// 17 PS9.5 right-clicking the rectangle's top edge in the view: Edit curve appearance….
// 18 the red swatch, ✓: only that line is red.
// 19 Part 6's Make transparent… (a view state, as Onshape's): it shows through.
// 20 PS9.7 the Appearances panel (the palette icon on the right): the parts (Part 2's face under
//    it), Extrude 1, the sketches (Sketch 2's red curve under it), each with its swatch.
// 21 PS9.7 double-clicking Part 1's row opens its Edit appearance dialog.
// 22 Esc, then one Undo (Ctrl+Z): the curve appearance is undone, the line is green again (the
//    last document change; Make transparent is a view state and not in the undo history).
// 23 PS2.10 the Variables icon: the Variables panel (its table, empty until variables exist)
//    replaces the Appearances panel; Configurations is greyed.
// 23a P3.11 (PS2.10) the Custom tables icon: the Custom tables panel replaces it (custom tables
//    come from FeatureScript table features, which cadrs doesn't have: it says so, its Add
//    button disabled).
// 24 (P3.6) hovering the greyed Configurations icon: its tooltip says it is not available
//    (configurations are out of scope, user decision 2026-09-29).
// 25 (P3.6) Part 6's context menu offers Make opaque (it is transparent); 26 chosen: opaque again.
